\notetitle{SLVP: Every Root of a Polynomial}

\begin{document}

\notehead

    \cmd{SLVP} takes the coefficients of a polynomial of any degree from a vector in \reg{X} and returns
    every root, real and complex, in one keystroke.  It completes a family: \cmd{SLVQ} solves the quadratic
    and \cmd{SLVC} the cubic, from stack registers or from the same highest-degree-first coefficient
    vector this note uses, and until now degree four was where the calculator stopped.  HP\,50g owners
    will recognise the shape of the function as \cmd{PROOT}, which has been the workhorse of control
    engineers and filter designers on that machine for twenty years.

    The first half of this note is about using the function: what it takes, what it returns, and six worked
    examples ending with an internal rate of return.  The second half is about what happens behind the
    keystroke: the companion matrix, the QR eigensolver that C47 already carries, what accuracy to expect
    and where the limits are.

    Numerical results in this document are of three kinds.  Results computed by hand are given with their
    working, so the reader can check them.  Results the C47 test suite asserts are identified by the file
    that asserts them, \file{src/testSuite/tests/slvp.txt}, which holds thirty-three cases covering every
    path through the function.  Independent confirmations were computed with 60-digit decimal arithmetic
    outside the calculator and are marked as such.  Nothing in this note has to be read off real hardware.

    \begin{warning}
    \cmd{SLVP} ships on the DM42n and the R47, and in the simulator.  It is not in any DM42 package: the
    DM42 flash images are full to within a kilobyte, and \cmd{SLVP} needs the eigensolver
    (\cmd{OPTION\_EIGEN}) beneath it.  On a DM42 the catalog shows \cmd{SLVP} struck through, which is the
    calculator's way of saying a function exists in the family but not in this build.
    \end{warning}

