\section{What SLVP Refuses}
\label{errors}

    Everything \cmd{SLVP} refuses is refused whole: \reg{X}, \reg{L} and the rest of the stack are exactly
    as they were, so a program can test and retry.  The suite asserts each row of Table \ref{tab:errors},
    including that the input survives.

    \begin{ntable}{@{}l l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.47\textwidth}@{}}{Every refusal, its error, and
             what triggers it.}{tab:errors}
        \hd{Input} & \hd{Error} & \hd{Why} \\ \midrule
        Not a matrix          & Invalid data type   & A scalar, string or integer in \reg{X}: the
                                                      coefficients must be a vector \\
        A matrix, not a vector & Matrix mismatch    & Both dimensions exceed 1 \\
        Degree 0              & Function domain     & A lone constant, an all-zero vector, or leading
                                                      zeros down to a constant: no degree, no roots \\
        Degree beyond memory  & RAM full            & The QR workspace grows as the square of the degree;
                                                      see Section \ref{limits} \\
    \end{ntable}

    \shot{05-error}{The degree-0 refusal: a $1\times1$ vector is a constant, and a constant has no roots.
    The vector is still in \reg{X}, untouched.}

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Behind the Keystroke}
\label{behind}

    \subsection{The companion matrix}

    \cmd{SLVP} contains no root-finding iteration of its own.  It normalises the polynomial to monic form
    at 75-digit precision, builds the companion matrix, and hands it to the shifted Householder-QR
    eigensolver that \cmd{EIGVAL} has used since it landed, working at the same 75 digits.  For the monic
    polynomial $x^d + b_{d-1}x^{d-1} + \cdots + b_0$ the companion matrix is

    \[
    C \;=\;
    \begin{pmatrix}
        0      & 1      &        &        \\
               & \ddots & \ddots &        \\
               &        & 0      & 1      \\
        -b_0   & -b_1   & \cdots & -b_{d-1}
    \end{pmatrix},
    \]

    and its eigenvalues are exactly the roots.  The eigensolver sorts them by descending magnitude and
    strips conjugate-pair noise, and \cmd{SLVP} rounds the 75-digit results to the 34 digits a register
    holds.  Degrees 1 and 2 inside the engine take closed forms rather than iterating, so small
    polynomials do not pay for machinery they do not need.

    The pipeline in order: validate, drop leading zeros, deflate trailing zeros, normalise to monic,
    special-case $x^d = w$, otherwise build the companion and iterate, then assemble, sort and type the
    result.  Only after all of that succeeds does anything touch the stack, which is what makes the
    refusals of Section \ref{errors} clean.

    \subsection{Why $x^d = w$ takes a different path}
    \label{circulant}

    The companion matrix of $x^d - w$ has ones on the superdiagonal and a single entry in the bottom row,
    and its eigenvalues all share one magnitude.  A shifted QR iteration lives on magnitude gaps to peel
    eigenvalues off, so this shape is the one polynomial the engine is poor at, and the engine screens it
    out rather than delivering slow garbage.  \cmd{SLVP} therefore solves two-term polynomials in closed
    form: magnitude $|w|^{1/d}$, angles $(\theta + 2\pi k)/d$, computed at 75 digits.  That is why the
    fifth roots of 32 arrive in angle order rather than sorted: all five magnitudes are equal by
    construction.

    The screen in the engine reads only the real parts of the bottom row.  A complex polynomial whose
    middle coefficients are all purely imaginary while its constant is real, like $x^2-3ix-2$ above,
    looks circulant to that screen although it is not a two-term polynomial at all.  \cmd{SLVP} sidesteps
    the false positive with a diagonal similarity transform: the companion is scaled so its superdiagonal
    is $\tfrac12$ instead of 1, which leaves every eigenvalue exactly where it was and makes the matrix
    invisible to the screen.  The suite asserts both quartic and quadratic cases through this path.

    \subsection{Accuracy}
    \label{accuracy}

    For well-separated roots the answer is simply correct to the display: the engine carries 75 digits and
    the register keeps 34.  The Wilkinson polynomial of degree 10, whose roots $1,\ldots,10$ are the
    classical stress test for coefficient sensitivity, comes back as the exact integers to all 34 digits;
    the suite asserts it.

    Repeated roots are different, and no algorithm escapes it: a root of multiplicity $m$ is determined by
    the coefficients only to about $75/m$ digits.  $(x-1)^4$ returns a tight cluster of four values within
    $3\times10^{-18}$ of 1, two of them carrying imaginary parts of about $7\times10^{-19}$, and the result
    is typed complex because that is what the numbers are.  The suite bakes these exact cluster values in,
    with a comment saying why, so any change to this behaviour will show.  Read a tight cluster with tiny
    imaginary parts as a repeated real root; the cluster diameter tells you the multiplicity's worth of
    digits you still have.

    One cleanup is applied on the way out, and only when every coefficient is real.  For a real
    polynomial a component more than 36 orders of magnitude below its root's dominant component is QR
    convergence residue, not data: a near-real conjugate pair $a\pm bi$ enters the real coefficients
    through $b^2$, which caps how small a genuine $b$ can be at roughly 17 orders below $a$.  Stripping
    the residue is what lets $x^2+1$ return $\pm i$ with the real parts exactly zero.  Complex
    coefficients carry independent exponents for the two parts, so a genuinely tiny component
    \emph{is} encodable there, no threshold is safe, and \cmd{SLVP} delivers the solver's raw values:
    the exact root $i$ of $x^4+ix^3-2$ arrives with a harmless $-4\times10^{-40}$ real part, which is
    the honest answer.

    \subsection{Degree limits}
    \label{limits}

    The QR workspace grows as the square of the degree, in 75-digit reals, taken from calculator memory
    and returned when done.  On the 256-KiB pool of the DM42n and R47 the practical ceiling is around
    degree 14; beyond it \cmd{SLVP} refuses with \lsc{ram full} before touching the stack, and the suite
    asserts a degree-20 refusal to pin the behaviour.  The two-term closed form of Section
    \ref{circulant} needs no matrix, so $x^{100}=1$ is fine.  The Wilkinson polynomial of degree 10
    solves in about nine hundred QR iterations; a repeated-root cluster drives the iteration to its cap
    and takes visibly longer, which is the hourglass you may see on a hard polynomial.

    \subsection{Command reference}

    \begin{ntable}{@{}l >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.62\textwidth}@{}}{\cmd{SLVP} at a glance.}{tab:ref}
        \hd{Takes}   & \reg{X}: real or complex vector, $1\times m$ or $m\times1$, $m\ge2$, highest-degree
                       coefficient first \\
        \hd{Leaves}  & \reg{X}: all $n$ roots as a $1\times n$ vector, descending magnitude, real if every
                       root is real, else complex \\
        \hd{Reads}   & nothing else \\
        \hd{Writes}  & \reg{L} (the coefficient vector); \cmd{CPXRES} set when the result is complex \\
        \hd{Refuses} & non-matrix, non-vector, degree 0, degree beyond memory; always with the stack
                       untouched (Table \ref{tab:errors}) \\
    \end{ntable}

