\section{SLVP in a Program}
\label{progentry}

    \cmd{SLVP} is an ordinary step.  The program this note used to prove the claim is three steps long:

    \prog{0001: LBL 'PSLV'\\0002: \ \ SLVP\\0003: END}

    Open a new program with \key{XEQ}\,\key{.}\,\key{.} and switch to program-entry mode with
    \fkey{PRGM}.  Start the label with \gkey{LBL}, then \fkey{$\alpha$}, type
    \key{P}\,\key{S}\,\key{L}\,\key{V}, and \key{ENTER}.  \cmd{SLVP} itself goes in from the catalog or
    from \fkey{ADV} exactly as it is run interactively.  Leave program-entry mode with \fkey{PRGM}.  With
    the coefficient vector in \reg{X}, \key{XEQ} \cmd{PSLV} returns the roots, and a wrong input mid-program
    halts with the same errors as Table \ref{tab:errors}.

% ===================================================================================================
\section{Availability and Cost}
\label{cost}

    \cmd{SLVP} is gated by \cmd{OPTION\_SLVP}, which requires \cmd{OPTION\_EIGEN} beneath it and is
    enabled for the DM42n, the R47 and the simulator.  All four DM42 packages exclude it; the measured
    cost of the option on the DM42n build, with the arm-gnu 13.2.Rel1 toolchain, is 2\,024 bytes of flash
    and no static RAM at all: every working byte is borrowed from calculator memory for the duration of
    the keystroke.  Two kilobytes for every root of every polynomial is the companion-matrix dividend:
    the expensive machinery was already on board, tested, and waiting.

